\section{Why course level, and what that costs}{
The register has no group, team or "Gruppe" column, so a participant cannot
be placed in group A rather than group B. Composition is therefore a
block-level covariate: in \code{y ~ card + (1 | block) + (1 | group)} it can
compete with the \strong{session} random effect and can never explain the
\strong{team} random effect. Every group inside one course gets the same row.
Fitting on this table is a between-course regression with as many points as
there are courses, and a covariate that barely varies between courses
cannot explain anything at all — check the spread before using one.
}

\section{The two halves of the missingness policy}{
Every covariate comes with three companions: \verb{n_<covariate>}, the number of
participants the course mean was taken over; \verb{n_imputed_<covariate>}, how
many of those numbers came from a participant-level fill rather than an
answer somebody wrote down (only the academic ladder has one by default);
and \verb{<covariate>_missing}, whether the course value itself was filled. The
two count columns always tell the truth, whatever \code{impute} is set to.
\itemize{
\item \code{impute = "flag"} (the default) fills a missing covariate with the
corpus-wide value — the pooled mean over \strong{all} participants, not the
mean of course means — and sets the indicator. A model can then carry
"this was missing" as information instead of pretending it was observed.
\item \code{impute = "median"} fills with the between-course median and does \strong{not}
set the indicator. It exists because a downstream pipeline can refuse a
